BT /F1 10 Tf 50 720 Td (IN THE MADRAS HIGH COURT \(MADURAI BENCH\)
AT MADURAI

CASE NO: H.C.P.\(MD\) No. 892 of 2026
CNR NUMBER: HCMD010456782026
DECISION DATE: 2026-02-27
YEAR: 2026

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Justice M.S. Ramesh & Justice M. Nirmal Kumar

PARTIES:
Kasthuri \(Wife of Detenu\)
... Appellant\(s\) / Petitioner\(s\)
Versus
The Additional Chief Secretary to Government, Home, Prohibition and Excise Department & District Magistrate, Tirunelveli
... Respondent\(s\) / State

STATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:
Tamil Nadu Act 14 of 1982 \(Goondas Act\), Constitution of India

SECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:
Tamil Nadu Act 14 of 1982 Section 3, Article 21, Article 22\(5\)

KEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:
Habeas Corpus, Preventive Detention, Goondas Act, Delay in Representation, Article 22\(5\)

================================================================================
                                   JUDGMENT
================================================================================

1. FACTUAL MATRIX:
Detenu was detained as 'Goonda' under TN Act 14/1982. Representation submitted by wife was kept pending for 24 days without any explanation by the Detaining Authority.

2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:
1. Whether unexplained delay in considering detenu representation vitiates preventive detention order under Article 22\(5\)?

3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:
Article 22\(5\) confers a constitutional right to have representation considered at the earliest opportunity as held in Rekha v. State of Tamil Nadu.

4. RATIO DECIDENDI:
Unexplained delay in considering representation of preventive detenu violates constitutional guarantee under Article 22\(5\), rendering detention illegal.

5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:
Habeas Corpus Petition Allowed; Detention order under Tamil Nadu Act 14 of 1982 quashed for unexplained 24-day delay in considering representation.

Decreed and ordered accordingly.

Hon'ble Justice M.S. Ramesh & Justice M. Nirmal Kumar
Madras High Court \(Ma) Tj ET
